\chapter{First-Order Terms, Unification, and Bounded Reduction}
\label{ch:terms-and-reduction}

A symbolic computation is a computation whose values are not numbers but
\emph{structures}: a syntax tree, an expression, a formula, a proof. The
operations on such values are not arithmetic but \emph{rewriting} --- the
replacement of one pattern by another --- and the questions one asks about
them are not numeric but structural: does this pattern match that value,
can these two patterns be made equal, what is the simplest form this
expression can be reduced to.

DSLtk provides the classical machinery for this. There is a term algebra, a
matcher, a unifier, a rewrite system with a bounded normaliser, and a
graph form of all of it in which repeated subterms are stored once and
shared. The graph form is the reason the whole facility exists in a
toolkit aimed at language construction, because sharing is what makes
large symbolic expressions --- and the equality-saturation machinery of
\chapref{ch:egraphs} --- tractable at all.

The facility is free-standing, in the same way the graph of
\chapref{ch:directed-graphs} is. The feature tags \code{dsl::Terms},
\code{dsl::TermRewriting} and \code{dsl::GraphReduction} are empty markers
with no mixin; \code{dsl::Term}, \code{dsl::TermRewriteSystem} and
\code{dsl::TermGraph} are ordinary classes and free functions that a
program uses directly.

\section{A worked example}

The program below exercises the whole facility. It starts with unification
and matching, moves on to a rewrite system over arithmetic expressions, then
contrasts the two rewriting strategies on a system where they disagree, and
finally performs the same rewrite over a shared graph and over an ordinary
tree in order to show what sharing buys.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Unification, rewriting, and the same rewrite over a shared graph.},label={lst:ch36-terms}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <string>

namespace
{

// Print the sequence of terms produced by repeated single-step rewriting.
void
show (const dsl::TermRewriteSystem &system, dsl::Term term,
      dsl::RewriteStrategy strategy, const char *label)
{
  std::cout << label << ':';
  while (auto next = system.rewrite_once (term, strategy))
    {
      term = next->term;
      std::cout << ' ' << term.dump ();
    }
  std::cout << '\n';
}

} // namespace

int
main ()
{
  // ---- unification and matching ---------------------------------------
  auto bound = dsl::unify_terms (dsl::parse_term ("(+ ?x ?y)"),
                                 dsl::parse_term ("(+ a b)"));
  if (bound)
    {
      std::cout << "unified:";
      for (auto &entry : *bound)
        std::cout << ' ' << entry.first << " = " << entry.second.dump ();
      std::cout << '\n';
    }

  std::cout << "match with a repeated variable: "
            << (dsl::match_term (dsl::parse_term ("(+ ?x ?x)"),
                                 dsl::parse_term ("(+ a a)"))
                    ? "yes"
                    : "no")
            << '\n';

  std::cout << "match with a clashing variable: "
            << (dsl::match_term (dsl::parse_term ("(+ ?x ?x)"),
                                 dsl::parse_term ("(+ a b)"))
                    ? "yes"
                    : "no")
            << '\n';

  std::cout << "occurs check rejects ?x = (f ?x): "
            << (dsl::unify_terms (dsl::parse_term ("?x"),
                                  dsl::parse_term ("(f ?x)"))
                    ? "accepted"
                    : "rejected")
            << '\n';

  // ---- a rewrite system over arithmetic terms -------------------------
  dsl::TermRewriteSystem arithmetic;
  arithmetic.add_rule ("(rewrite add-zero (+ 0 ?x) ?x)");
  arithmetic.add_rule ("(rewrite mul-zero (* 0 ?x) 0)");
  arithmetic.add_rule ("(rewrite mul-one (* 1 ?x) ?x)");
  arithmetic.add_rule ("(rewrite add-assoc (+ ?x (+ ?y ?z)) (+ (+ ?x ?y) ?z))");

  auto term = dsl::parse_term ("(+ (* 1 (+ 0 3)) (* 0 5))");
  auto outer
      = arithmetic.normalize (term, 100, dsl::RewriteStrategy::Outermost);
  std::cout << "outermost: " << outer.term.dump () << " in " << outer.steps
            << " steps, normal form: "
            << (outer.normal_form () ? "yes" : "no") << '\n';

  // ---- outermost and innermost choose different redexes ----------------
  dsl::TermRewriteSystem strategies;
  strategies.add_rule ("(rewrite f-to-p (f a) p)");
  strategies.add_rule ("(rewrite a-to-b a b)");

  auto subject = dsl::parse_term ("(f a)");
  show (strategies, subject, dsl::RewriteStrategy::Outermost,
        "outermost sequence");
  show (strategies, subject, dsl::RewriteStrategy::Innermost,
        "innermost sequence");

  // ---- the same rewrite over a shared graph ----------------------------
  dsl::TermGraph graph;
  auto root = graph.add ("(+ (f a) (f a))");
  std::cout << "graph nodes for the shared term: "
            << graph.graph ().node_count () << '\n';
  graph.add ("(g b)");

  dsl::TermRewriteSystem collapse;
  collapse.add_rule ("(rewrite f-a (f a) a)");

  auto step = graph.rewrite_once (root, collapse);
  std::cout << "after one step: "
            << graph.to_term (step ? step->root : root).dump () << '\n';

  graph.collect_garbage ({root});
  std::cout << "nodes after collection: " << graph.graph ().node_count ()
            << '\n';

  // ---- the same rewrite over an ordinary tree --------------------------
  dsl::TermRewriteSystem plain;
  plain.add_rule ("(rewrite f-a (f a) a)");
  auto plain_term = dsl::parse_term ("(+ (f a) (f a))");
  auto plain_result = plain.normalize (plain_term, 100);
  std::cout << "tree version: " << plain_result.term.dump () << " in "
            << plain_result.steps << " steps, " << plain_term.size ()
            << " nodes\n";

  std::cout << graph.to_dot ("shared") << '\n';
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch36-terms}.},label={lst:ch36-output}]
unified: x = a y = b
match with a repeated variable: yes
match with a clashing variable: no
occurs check rejects ?x = (f ?x): rejected
outermost: (+ 3 0) in 3 steps, normal form: yes
outermost sequence: p
innermost sequence: (f b)
graph nodes for the shared term: 3
after one step: (+ a a)
nodes after collection: 2
tree version: (+ a a) in 2 steps, 5 nodes
digraph "shared" {
  n2 [label="+"];
  n5 [label="a"];
  n2 -> n5 [label="0"];
  n2 -> n5 [label="1"];
}
\end{lstlisting}

The last few lines are the heart of the chapter. The tree version of
\code{(+ (f a) (f a))} has five nodes, because the two occurrences of
\code{(f a)} are stored separately. The graph version has three, because
the two occurrences are the same node. When the rule \code{(f a) -> a} is
applied, the tree version needs two steps --- one for each occurrence ---
and the graph version needs one, because rewriting the shared node updates
both parents at once. This is the entire point of the graph form, and it
is what makes symbolic manipulation of large expressions feasible.

