\section{Terms}

A term is either a variable or a function application. The
\code{is_variable} flag distinguishes them, and the invariant is that a
variable never has arguments. \code{validate ()} enforces it, throwing
\code{"variable cannot have arguments"} if it does not hold, and it is
called at the entry to every operation that takes a term, so an ill-formed
term is rejected rather than silently mishandled.

The distinction between a variable and a zero-argument function is worth
dwelling on, because the textual syntax makes it easy to conflate them. An
unquoted atom that begins with a question mark is a variable, and the
question mark is stripped: \code{?x} is the variable \code{x}. Every other
atom is a \emph{function} with no arguments, so \code{a} is the constant
function \code{a}, not a variable. A list must begin with a literal,
non-variable atom, or the conversion fails with
\code{"term requires a literal function symbol"}: a term cannot have a
variable in function position.

\code{parse_term} puts the two together: it parses an S-expression with
\code{parse_sexpr} and converts it with \code{term_from_sexpr}. The
rendering goes the other way through \code{Term::sexpr}, which produces
\code{?name} for a variable and a list headed by the symbol for a
function. A function whose symbol happens to begin with a question mark is
emitted as a \emph{quoted} atom, so that \code{dump} and \code{parse_term}
round-trip correctly.

Navigation is by path. \code{at} takes a span of argument indices and walks
down the tree, using \code{std::vector::at} so that an invalid path throws
\code{std::out_of_range}. \code{replaced} takes the same kind of path and
returns a copy of the term with the addressed sub-term replaced; it is
implemented as a copy-then-write, so the original term is untouched. An
empty path replaces the whole term, which makes \code{replaced} usable as a
general substitution primitive as well as a sub-term editor.

\section{Matching and unification}

The two operations look similar and are not.

\emph{Matching} is one-way. \code{match_term (pattern, subject)} asks
whether the pattern can be seen in the subject, binding the pattern's
variables as it goes. The subject is never bound. This is the operation a
rewrite rule needs, because a rule says \emph{this shape on the left may be
replaced by that shape on the right}, and the right-hand side may only
mention variables that the left-hand side determined.

\emph{Unification} is two-way. \code{unify_terms (left, right)} asks
whether the two terms can be made equal by binding variables on either
side, and returns a most general unifier. This is the operation a
constraint solver or a logical-programming matcher needs.

The difference shows up immediately in the example. Matching \code{(+ ?x
?x)} against \code{(+ a b)} fails, because the two occurrences of
\code{?x} would have to bind to different values. Unifying \code{(+ ?x
?y)} with \code{(+ a b)} succeeds, with \code{?x} bound to \code{a} and
\code{?y} to \code{b}.

The check that makes the first case fail is worth naming, because it is
easy to overlook. When a pattern variable has already been bound, the
matcher does not simply fail; it compares the previous binding with the
new value and succeeds only if they are equal. That comparison \emph{is}
the occurrence check for repeated variables --- the matcher does not need a
separate mechanism, because a repeated variable is exactly a variable that
is asked to bind twice.

Unification is more elaborate, and its implementation is a stack of term
pairs rather than a recursive walk.

\begin{pseudocode}[Unifying two terms]
\textbf{function} unify (left, right)\\
\quad bindings = an empty substitution\\
\quad pending = a stack holding the pair (left, right)\\
\quad \textbf{while} pending is not empty\\
\qquad \qquad first, second = pop from pending\\
\qquad \qquad first = substitute (first, bindings)\\
\qquad \qquad second = substitute (second, bindings)\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{if} first equals second then continue the loop\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{if} second is a variable and first is not\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad swap first and second\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{if} first is a variable\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{if} second contains the variable first\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \textbf{return} failure\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad binding = first mapped to second\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad \textbf{for} each entry in bindings\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad apply binding to the entry\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad add binding to bindings\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{if} the symbols differ then \textbf{return} failure\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{if} the arities differ then \textbf{return} failure\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{for} each index\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad push the pair of children onto pending\\
\quad \textbf{return} bindings
\end{pseudocode}

Four features of this algorithm are worth drawing out.

The pairs on the stack are \emph{re-substituted} with the current bindings
before they are compared. This is what keeps the algorithm correct when a
binding made earlier changes the shape of a term that is still waiting to
be processed.

When exactly one side of a pair is a variable the two are swapped, so that
the variable is always on the left. The implementation swaps only when the
second side is a variable and the first is not; when both are variables
the first is bound to the second, which is harmless because two identical
variables are caught by the equality test at the top of the loop.

The \emph{occurs check} rejects a binding of a variable to a term that
contains it. Binding \code{?x} to \code{(f ?x)} would define an infinite
term, so the unification fails. Note that this check is present in
unification but \emph{not} in matching, and it does not need to be:
matching never builds a term, so it cannot create an infinite one.

Finally, the new binding is applied to every existing binding \emph{before}
it is inserted. That keeps the substitution idempotent: after binding
\code{?x} to \code{a} and then \code{?y} to \code{?x}, the substitution
maps \code{?y} to \code{a} rather than to \code{?x}, so applying the
substitution twice gives the same answer as applying it once.

\section{Rules and strategies}

A \code{TermRule} is a left-hand side, a right-hand side and a name. Its
\code{validate ()} enforces two conditions: the left-hand side may not be
a variable, and every variable occurring on the right-hand side must also
occur on the left. The errors are \code{"rewrite left side cannot be a
variable"} and \code{"unbound rewrite variable"}. The second condition is
what makes a rule's right-hand side computable: a rule that mentions a
variable its left-hand side never binds could never be instantiated.

\code{term_rule} parses a rule from a string using the same S-expression
syntax as the e-graph rewrite rules of \chapref{ch:egraphs}: the form is
\code{(rewrite name lhs rhs)}, and variables are written \code{?name}. The
listing uses this form throughout.

\code{TermRewriteSystem} holds the rules in insertion order and offers two
operations. \code{rewrite_once} performs a single step and returns the
new term together with the index of the rule that fired and the position
of the redex. \code{normalize} repeats it until no rule applies or until
the step limit is reached.

The strategy decides \emph{which} redex is chosen. \code{Outermost} tries
the root of the term first and descends into the children only if no rule
matches the root. \code{Innermost} descends first and tries a node only
after all of its children have failed. The effect is that the innermost
strategy selects the leftmost-deepest redex and the outermost strategy
selects the leftmost-outermost one.

The two strategies can genuinely disagree, and the listing demonstrates it
with a tiny system. The rules are \code{(f a) -> p} and \code{a -> b}, and
the subject is \code{(f a)}. The outermost strategy rewrites the whole
term at once and produces \code{p}. The innermost strategy descends into
the argument first, rewrites \code{a} to \code{b}, and is left with
\code{(f b)}, which matches neither rule. The two strategies have produced
different normal forms from the same input, and the difference is not an
accident of the implementation: it is the definition of the two
strategies.

\begin{pseudocode}[Normalizing a term]
\textbf{function} normalize (term, max\_steps, strategy)\\
\quad steps = 0\\
\quad \textbf{loop}\\
\qquad \qquad next = rewrite\_once (term, strategy)\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{if} next does not exist then leave the loop\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{if} steps equals max\_steps\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{return} the term, the steps, and a step limit\\
\qquad \qquad term = next.term\\
\qquad \qquad steps = steps plus 1\\
\quad \textbf{return} the term, the steps, and a normal form
\end{pseudocode}

The order of the two tests in that loop matters. The limit is checked
\emph{after} a successful rewrite but \emph{before} the rewritten term is
adopted, so at most \code{max_steps} rewrites are ever applied, and the
term returned when the limit is hit is the one that was reached before the
last rewrite. The default limit is 1000.

The step limit is the only protection against a rule set that does not
terminate. A rule such as \code{f(?x) -> f(?x)} would loop for ever, and
\code{normalize} would return a result with \code{stop} set to
\code{StepLimit} rather than hanging.

\code{TermRewriteStep} carries the position of the redex as a vector of
argument indices, which is exactly the path format that \code{Term::at} and
\code{Term::replaced} accept. A caller that wants to know \emph{where} in
the original term the rewrite happened --- to build a source map, or to
highlight the changed region in an editor --- can therefore take the
position straight from the step and use it with the term navigation
functions. The rule index is carried for the same reason: it identifies
which of the system's rules fired, which is usually what a diagnostic
message needs to name.

