\label{fig:ch36-dag}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:ch36-dag} shows what sharing means. The term
\code{(+ (f a) (f a))} has five nodes as a tree, because the two
occurrences of \code{(f a)} are distinct. Interned into a graph it has
three, because the two occurrences are the same node.

\code{TermGraph} is a thin wrapper around a
\code{DirectedGraph<TermGraphLabel, std::size_t>}. The node label is a
symbol and an \code{is_variable} flag; the edge value is the index of the
child within its parent, which is what lets the DOT export label the edges
with their positions.

Building a graph from a term is \code{add (term, share = true)}. The
interning is bottom-up, so identical sub-terms within the added term
become the same node, and the intern table is seeded from the nodes that
already exist, so an added term can reuse them. The seeding uses
\code{emplace}, which does not overwrite, so the first node to claim a key
keeps it. Passing \code{share = false} disables interning entirely and
builds a fresh node for every occurrence.

Matching over a graph is the analogue of \code{match_term}. A pattern
variable binds to a \emph{node identifier} rather than to a term, and the
occurrence check for a repeated variable is a structural comparison of two
nodes. That comparison has to be \emph{co-inductive}, because the graph may
be cyclic: \code{equal_nodes} compares a pair of nodes by pushing their
children pairs onto a work list and remembering, in a map from the left
identifier to the set of right identifiers already compared, which pairs
have been seen. A pair that has been seen is assumed equal, which is
exactly right for cyclic structures and is what makes the comparison
terminate.

Finding a redex walks the graph with the depth-first traversal of
\chapref{ch:directed-graphs}, in pre-order for the outermost strategy and
in post-order for the innermost one, and tries the rules in order at each
node. Applying a redex is where the graph form earns its keep.

\begin{pseudocode}[Applying a rewrite to a shared graph]
\textbf{function} apply (root, system, candidate)\\
\quad replacement = instantiate (system.rhs, candidate.bindings)\\
\quad redirect (candidate.id, replacement)\\
\quad remove (candidate.id)\\
\quad \textbf{if} candidate.id is the root\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{return} the replacement as the new root\\
\quad \textbf{return} the unchanged root
\end{pseudocode}

\code{redirect} is the operation described in
\chapref{ch:directed-graphs}: it retargets the edges pointing into the
redex so that they point into the replacement, and leaves everything else
alone. The redex is then removed. Because the redex may be shared, every
parent that pointed at it is updated at once, which is why the graph
version of the example needs one step where the tree version needs two.

The redex's own children are \emph{discarded}. The edge from the redex to
its children dies with the redex; they are not re-attached to the
replacement. That is the correct semantics for rewriting --- a rule that
replaces a term replaces the whole term, arguments included --- but it does
mean that a graph rewrite cannot be used to splice the children of the
replaced node onto its replacement.

\code{collect_garbage (roots)} removes every node that is not reachable
from the given roots, using the depth-first traversal to compute the
reachable set. It is the graph form's counterpart to letting a tree fall out
of scope, and it has to be called explicitly.

\code{to_term (root)} converts a graph back into a tree, expanding the
sharing. It is the operation that undoes \code{add}, and it is the point at
which the benefit of sharing is paid back: the resulting tree may be much
larger than the graph it came from.

The graph form is not a different algebra; it is the same one with an
implementation detail changed. \code{to_term} produces a \code{Term} that
is equal, by \code{Term}'s structural comparison, to the term that was
added --- provided the graph is acyclic. A program can therefore mix the two
freely: build a graph, rewrite it, and convert the result back to a tree
when a tree-shaped interface is wanted. The caveat is that the conversion
recurses once per level of the graph, so a cyclic graph must never be
converted.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{warnbox}[Terms are trees and trees duplicate]
\code{dsl::Term} is a value type with deep copies. A term that mentions the
same sub-term twice stores it twice, and every operation that copies the
term copies both copies. For an expression of size \emph{n} whose
structure repeats, the tree representation can be exponentially larger
than the graph representation, and the copying cost of \code{replaced} and
\code{normalize} is paid on the tree, not on the structure the user
cares about. This is the single most important reason to prefer
\code{TermGraph} for anything large.
\end{warnbox}

The remaining limits are these.

\textbf{Variables are names, not bindings.} A variable is a string and a
flag. There is no scoping, no typing, no arity checking and no distinction
between a bound and a free variable. The term algebra is first-order and
nothing more.

\textbf{There is no occurs check in matching.} Matching \code{?x} against
a term containing \code{?x} succeeds, and that is correct: matching binds
variables to existing terms and never constructs anything. The occurs check
exists only in unification, where a binding could otherwise define an
infinite term.

\textbf{Unification can be expensive.} The algorithm applies each new
binding to every existing binding, which is quadratic in the number of
bindings in the worst case, and the stack of pending pairs can grow
proportionally to the size of the terms. There is no union-find, no
tri-state unification, and no occurs check that is cheaper than a variable
scan.

\textbf{Rules have no priorities and no weights.} They are tried in
insertion order, and the first one that matches wins. A grammar with
overlapping left-hand sides will therefore behave in a way that depends on
the order the rules were added.

\textbf{Normalisation may not terminate.} The step limit is the only
protection, and it defaults to 1000. A rule set that needs more steps
than the limit will be reported as \code{StepLimit} rather than as a normal
form, and the caller has to distinguish the two with
\code{normal_form ()}.

\textbf{The graph form discards the redex's children.} As described above,
applying a rewrite in \code{TermGraph} removes the redex node together
with its outgoing edges. A user who wants the children to survive has to
rebuild the edges.

\textbf{\code{collect_garbage} trusts its argument.} It removes every node
unreachable from the supplied roots. Passing the wrong roots --- or an
empty vector --- deletes live structure, and there is no way to recover it.

\textbf{\code{to_term} can be much larger than the graph.} Expanding a
shared graph into a tree duplicates every shared sub-term. A graph with
exponential sharing becomes a tree with exponential size.

\textbf{Variables in a graph are shared like everything else.} Interning
does not distinguish a variable node from a function node, so two
occurrences of the same variable name become the same node. This is
harmless for the intended uses, but it means that a graph cannot represent
two \emph{distinct} variables that happen to share a name.

\section{Closing}

The term facility completes DSLtk's collection of symbolic data
structures. \code{dsl::Term} is the tree form: a plain value type with
structural equality, path-based navigation, and a printer. On top of it sit
the two matching operations --- one-way \code{match_term} for rewrite
rules, two-way \code{unify_terms} with an occurs check for constraint
solving --- and a rewrite system whose normaliser is bounded by a step
count and whose strategy is explicit.

\code{dsl::TermGraph} is the same algebra with sharing, and it is the form
that matters for anything large. Interning collapses repeated sub-terms
into single nodes; rewriting a shared node updates every parent at once;
and the comparison of two graph nodes is co-inductive, so cyclic structures
can be compared without hanging. The pair of facilities is what allows the
equality-saturation machinery of \chapref{ch:egraphs} to be practical, and
it is where DSLtk's support for symbolic computation ends.

What remains is the D port of the toolkit, which is the subject of the next
chapter.
